headers {
  HX-Request: true
  HX-Current-URL: {{baseUrl}}/
}

docs {
  # go-tiimit API collection

  Manual testing for the fragment/action endpoints — the ones HTMX calls,
  not the full page routes.

  ## Setup

  1. Run the app: `make dev` (server listens on 127.0.0.1:8080).
  2. Pick the `local` environment.
  3. Run **auth > Login** once. Bruno stores the `user_session_id`
     cookie and sends it on every later request in this collection.
  4. Set `playerId`, `practiceId` and `myClubId` in the environment to
     values that exist in your local DB.

  ## The HX-Request header

  Set at collection level so every request looks like an HTMX call.
  It matters in two places:

  - `requireAuth` returns `401` + `HX-Redirect: /login` for HTMX requests
    instead of a `303` redirect.
  - Nothing else branches on it today, but the handlers return HTML
    fragments that only make sense as an HTMX swap.

  Remove the header on a request (Headers tab) to see the non-HTMX path.

  ## renderError vs renderOK

  `handleSetPlayerInactive`, `handleViewPlayersPage` and
  `handleAddPlayerForm` use the `renderError`/`renderOK` helpers so far;
  the rollout to the remaining handlers is in progress. Telling them apart
  in a response:

  | Signal | renderError | renderOK |
  |---|---|---|
  | `HX-Reswap: none` response header | present | absent |
  | Body | just the `#app-error` div, populated | the real fragment + an empty `#app-error` div |

  Both return `200 OK` — the status code is not the signal, the
  `HX-Reswap` header is. The tests on those requests assert exactly that.

  Other handlers still signal failure the old way: a bare
  `w.WriteHeader(4xx/5xx)` with an empty body, or silently returning
  `200` with nothing at all. Requests below note which behaviour to expect.
}
